 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: Generally $O(N \cdot M)$ where $N$ is the number of triangles and $M$ is the number of voxels covered by each triangle, but optimized by rasterization to $O(N \cdot A)$ where $A$ is the screen area in voxels [S1][S4, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(V)$ where $V$ is the number of occupied voxels, typically stored in a hash table for sparse grids [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Performance**: GPU-based implementations offer significant speedups (e.g., 50x) for high-resolution grids compared to CPU-based methods [S1].
-*   **Strengths**: Fast, leverages existing graphics hardware, suitable for real-time applications [S1][S5, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations**: Can produce holes or cracks if not using conservative rasterization [S5, unofficial]. Output mesh size can be very large compared to input [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use**: When real-time voxelization is required, or when the input is a triangle mesh and a voxelized representation is needed for rendering or simulation [S1][S4, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use**: When exact analytical surface representation is needed, or when memory bandwidth is a critical bottleneck and sparse structures are preferred [S4, S5, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N \cdot B)$, where $N$ is the number of triangles and $B$ is the average number of voxels in the triangle's bounding box. In GPU implementations, this is parallelized, reducing effective time to $O(N)$ or $O(V)$ depending on implementation [S1][S5, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(V)$, where $V$ is the number of occupied voxels. Hash tables add overhead for sparse grids [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Performance**: GPU-based methods offer significant speedups (e.g., 50x) for high-resolution grids compared to CPU-based nested loops [S1].
+*   **Strengths**: Fast, leverages existing graphics hardware, handles complex meshes well [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations**: Output mesh size can be very large (scaling with voxel density) [S2, unofficial]. Standard rasterization can produce holes [S5, unofficial]. Conservative rasterization can overfill [S5, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use**: Real-time applications, voxel art generation, preprocessing for volume rendering [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use**: When exact analytical surface representation is required, or when memory bandwidth is severely constrained and sparse structures are preferred [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Rasterization-based voxelization has been widely used in computer graphics for converting polygonal models into volumetric data for rendering, simulation, and 3D printing [S1][S2, unofficial]. The method leverages the efficiency of scan-conversion algorithms, which are fundamental to raster graphics [S4, unofficial]. Libraries such as Point-Cloud-Utils and various header-only C libraries have popularized efficient implementations [S1][S3, unofficial]. The technique has evolved to include GPU acceleration via compute shaders and hardware rasterization pipelines to handle real-time requirements [S1][S5, unofficial].
+Rasterization-based voxelization originated from the need to efficiently convert polygonal models into volumetric data for rendering and simulation [S1][S4, unofficial]. Early implementations used CPU-based scanline algorithms [S1]. The method evolved with GPU hardware, leveraging fragment shaders and later compute shaders for parallel processing [S2, S5, unofficial]. Libraries like Point-Cloud-Utils and the C-based voxelizer library popularized simple, header-only implementations for game development and voxel art [S1][S2, unofficial]. It is widely used in voxel art tools, real-time ray tracing preprocessing, and volume rendering pipelines [S1][S4, S5, unofficial].
 
